---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<!-- contact-v2 -->
<BaseLayout
  title="Contact"
  description="Contact Eric Nolan and find his public AI engineering and product work."
>
  <header class="subhero subhero--contact">
    <div class="shell contact-hero-grid">
      <div class="subhero-copy">
        <div class="subhero-badges">
          <span>AI DEVELOPMENT</span>
          <span>TECHNICAL PRODUCT</span>
          <span>OPEN TO CONVERSATION</span>
        </div>
        <p class="subhero-kicker">CONTACT // LET'S TALK ABOUT THE HARD PART</p>
        <h1>Bring me the problem that doesn't fit neatly in a box.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          AI systems, developer workflows, product architecture, operational friction, governance, or a messy process nobody wants to own—those are usually the interesting conversations.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="contact-panel">
        <a class="contact-action contact-action--github" href="https://github.com/unyieldingclaw-dev">
          <span>INSPECT THE WORK</span>
          <strong>GitHub</strong>
          <small>Public repos, implementation evidence, research →</small>
        </a>
        <a class="contact-action contact-action--email" href="mailto:unyieldingclaw@gmail.com">
          <span>START THE CONVERSATION</span>
          <strong>Email</strong>
          <small>unyieldingclaw@gmail.com →</small>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section contact-fit">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading section-heading--punch">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Good conversation starters</p>
          <h2>What I like working through.</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="section-intro">
          I am especially interested in the point where product intent, AI behavior, engineering constraints, and operating reality collide.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="contact-topic-grid">
        <article><span>01</span><strong>AI product architecture</strong><p>How the model, context, tools, state, APIs, governance, and user workflow fit together.</p></article>
        <article><span>02</span><strong>Developer productivity</strong><p>Tools and workflows that help engineers without quietly creating another source of friction.</p></article>
        <article><span>03</span><strong>Bounded automation</strong><p>Where AI should act, where it should ask, and where deterministic controls should take over.</p></article>
        <article><span>04</span><strong>Messy operational systems</strong><p>Release, intake, handoffs, visibility, recovery, and the recurring manual work everyone has learned to tolerate.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--dark contact-boundary">
    <div class="shell bridge-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Public-safe on purpose</p>
        <h2>Evidence without oversharing.</h2>
        <p class="section-intro">
          This site does not publish employer-internal code, confidential implementations, credentials, customer data, or private-project user data just to make the portfolio look more complete.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="bridge-card">
        <span>THE RULE</span>
        <strong>Show enough to demonstrate judgment. Keep private what should remain private.</strong>
        <small>That boundary is part of engineering maturity too.</small>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
